From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hefesto DualSense4Unix Project <hefesto-dualsense4unix@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 12:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] HID: playstation: allow retrying feature reports that time out
 during probe

Losing one feature report during probe costs the user the whole controller,
and over bluetooth that happens for reasons unrelated to the controller being
broken. Measured with two DualSense pads connecting to the same adapter about
a second apart. The first completed probe in 74 ms. The second retrieved its
pairing info (reportID 9) and then lost the firmware info (reportID 32):

  playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0008: Registered DualSense controller ...
  playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0009: Failed to retrieve feature with reportID 32: -5
  playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0009: Failed to retrieve DualSense firmware info: -5
  playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0009: Failed to create dualsense.
  playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0009: probe with driver playstation failed with error -5

The -5 hides what happened. Over bluetooth hid_hw_raw_request() lands in uhid,
and uhid_hid_get_report() turns any error the transport reports into -EIO:

  if (req->err)
          ret = -EIO;

Three seconds passed between the request and that -EIO, which is not uhid's
own 5 second wait. The daemon on the other end recorded the real error:

  bluetoothd: profiles/input/device.c:hidp_report_req_timeout()
              Device <addr> HIDP GET_REPORT request timed out

That is BlueZ's REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT, three seconds, on the HID control
channel, answered to the kernel as ETIMEDOUT. So the controller was reachable
- it had answered the previous feature report - it just did not get the
control channel within three seconds while a second pad was being brought up
on the same radio. The failure scales with the number of controllers pairing
at once, and four is a normal number of players.

Reading a feature report is a pure read, so repeating it is safe. By the time
the timeout fires the transport has already cleared its pending request, so
the retry is accepted immediately rather than rejected as busy. Probe runs in
a uhid worker - uhid schedules hid_add_device() through one specifically so
that drivers may issue feature requests from .probe - so sleeping between
attempts does not block the daemon that has to answer them.

All the failures __ps_get_report() detects are retried, not only the transport
error: a short transfer, a mismatched reportID and a bad CRC all describe a
transfer that did not arrive intact, which is the same transient.

The spacing between attempts is the whole of it, and getting it wrong is the
same as not retrying at all. This was first written waiting 100 ms and then
200 ms, and the arithmetic was never done: a failed attempt over bluetooth has
already spent the full REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT, so three attempts 100 ms and 200 ms
apart are three questions asked inside one contention window. Measured at
00:17:04, :07 and :10 followed by the abort, and over 2026-08-08 six of six
probes that retried still aborted, not one of them saved. The retry only made
the failure cost ~10 s instead of ~3.3 s.

So the backoff is written in units of the timeout it exists to outlive. It
starts at twice REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT, which is the smallest wait that asks the
question in a window the previous attempt has not already proven contended,
doubles, and is capped at four times it so that a large feature_retries cannot
park a probe worker for minutes. Over USB none of this applies - no BlueZ is
in the path, a failed attempt returns at once, and the failure measured there
is a clone answering the same short report every time - so that side keeps the
original 100 ms.

Defaults to a single attempt, i.e. current behavior.

Signed-off-by: Hefesto DualSense4Unix Project <hefesto-dualsense4unix@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c | 118 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 116 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

index 9c8b0f0..a71e3d5 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/bits.h>
 #include <linux/cleanup.h>
 #include <linux/crc32.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/hid.h>
 #include <linux/idr.h>
@@ -21,6 +22,45 @@

 #include "hid-ids.h"

+/*
+ * Feature reports are read during probe only, and over bluetooth a single
+ * unanswered one costs the whole controller. See ps_get_report() for the
+ * measured failure this parameter exists for.
+ */
+static unsigned int feature_retries;
+module_param(feature_retries, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(feature_retries,
+		 "Extra attempts when a feature report read fails during probe, for controllers that lose the race for the bluetooth control channel when several pair at once (default 0 = one attempt, same as before)");
+
+/*
+ * BlueZ answers a GET_REPORT on the HID control channel inside its own
+ * REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT (profiles/input/device.c, hidp_report_req_timeout()) or
+ * not at all, and that timeout is three seconds. Every wait below is written
+ * as a multiple of it, because it is the clock a failed attempt is really
+ * paying, and the one a retry has to outlive to mean anything.
+ */
+#define PS_BLUEZ_REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT_MS	3000
+
+/*
+ * Backoff before the first retry over bluetooth, and the ceiling for the
+ * doubling. Both are multiples of the timeout above, and the first one is
+ * deliberately larger than it: an attempt that timed out has proven the
+ * control channel was contended for one whole REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT window, so
+ * sleeping for less than that window asks the same question inside it. See
+ * ps_get_report() for what was measured when this used to be 100 ms.
+ */
+#define PS_FEATURE_RETRY_DELAY_MS	(2U * PS_BLUEZ_REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT_MS)
+#define PS_FEATURE_RETRY_MAX_DELAY_MS	(4U * PS_BLUEZ_REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT_MS)
+
+/*
+ * Over USB there is no BlueZ in the path, so none of the above applies: a
+ * failed attempt costs nothing and comes back at once. The failure measured
+ * on that side is a clone answering the pairing info report short, with the
+ * same bytes every time, so spacing the attempts buys nothing and only delays
+ * the fallback that does help. Keep the original 100 ms.
+ */
+#define PS_FEATURE_RETRY_USB_DELAY_MS	100
+
 /* List of connected playstation devices. */
 static DEFINE_MUTEX(ps_devices_lock);
 static LIST_HEAD(ps_devices_list);
@@ -787,8 +827,8 @@
 	return gamepad;
 }

-static int ps_get_report(struct hid_device *hdev, u8 report_id, u8 *buf,
-			 size_t size, bool check_crc)
+static int __ps_get_report(struct hid_device *hdev, u8 report_id, u8 *buf,
+			   size_t size, bool check_crc)
 {
 	int ret;

@@ -823,6 +863,80 @@
 	return 0;
 }

+/*
+ * A feature report that goes unanswered once costs the user the whole
+ * controller, and over bluetooth that happens for reasons that have nothing to
+ * do with the controller being broken.
+ *
+ * Measured with two DualSense pads connecting to the same adapter about a
+ * second apart. The first completed probe in 74 ms. The second got its pairing
+ * info (reportID 9) fine and then lost the firmware info (reportID 32):
+ *
+ *   playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0009: Failed to retrieve feature with reportID 32: -5
+ *   playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0009: Failed to retrieve DualSense firmware info: -5
+ *   playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0009: Failed to create dualsense.
+ *   playstation 0005:054C:0CE6.0009: probe with driver playstation failed with error -5
+ *
+ * The -5 is not what happened. Over bluetooth hid_hw_raw_request() goes to
+ * uhid, and uhid flattens every error the userspace transport reports into
+ * -EIO. Three seconds elapsed between the request and the failure, and the
+ * daemon on the other side said what it really was:
+ *
+ *   bluetoothd: profiles/input/device.c:hidp_report_req_timeout()
+ *               Device <addr> HIDP GET_REPORT request timed out
+ *
+ * i.e. BlueZ's own REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT (3 s) on the HID control channel, sent
+ * back as ETIMEDOUT and delivered to the driver as -EIO. The controller was
+ * reachable - it had just answered the previous feature report - it simply did
+ * not win the control channel within three seconds while a second pad was
+ * being brought up on the same radio.
+ *
+ * That is a transient the driver can ride out. Reading a feature report is a
+ * pure read with no side effects, so repeating it is safe, and by the time the
+ * timeout fires the transport has already cleared its pending request, so the
+ * retry is accepted immediately. Probe runs in a uhid worker (uhid schedules
+ * hid_add_device() precisely so drivers may issue feature requests from
+ * .probe), so waiting here does not block the daemon that has to answer.
+ *
+ * Every failure __ps_get_report() detects - transport error, short transfer,
+ * wrong reportID, bad CRC - describes a transfer that did not arrive intact,
+ * so all of them are retried.
+ *
+ * Off by default: one attempt, exactly as before.
+ *
+ * The spacing is not free-standing. This first shipped retrying after 100 ms
+ * and 200 ms, which is the same as not retrying at all, and the arithmetic
+ * says why: over bluetooth a failed attempt has already spent the whole
+ * REPORT_REQ_TIMEOUT, so three attempts 100 ms and 200 ms apart are three
+ * questions asked inside one contention episode. Measured in one journal at
+ * 00:17:04, :07 and :10, then probe aborted; over 2026-08-08 six of six
+ * probes that retried still aborted and not one was saved. All the retry
+ * bought was a failure that took ~10 s instead of ~3.3 s. The backoff is now
+ * read as a multiple of the timeout it has to ride over, and the budget is
+ * spent on spacing rather than on more attempts crowded into one window.
+ */
+static int ps_get_report(struct hid_device *hdev, u8 report_id, u8 *buf,
+			 size_t size, bool check_crc)
+{
+	unsigned int attempts_left = min(feature_retries, 10U) + 1;
+	unsigned int delay_ms = hdev->bus == BUS_BLUETOOTH ?
+				PS_FEATURE_RETRY_DELAY_MS :
+				PS_FEATURE_RETRY_USB_DELAY_MS;
+	int ret;
+
+	for (;;) {
+		ret = __ps_get_report(hdev, report_id, buf, size, check_crc);
+		if (!ret || --attempts_left == 0)
+			return ret;
+
+		hid_warn(hdev,
+			 "retrying feature reportID %d in %u ms (%u attempt(s) left)\n",
+			 report_id, delay_ms, attempts_left);
+		msleep(delay_ms);
+		delay_ms = min(delay_ms * 2, PS_FEATURE_RETRY_MAX_DELAY_MS);
+	}
+}
+
 static int ps_led_register(struct ps_device *ps_dev, struct led_classdev *led,
 			   const struct ps_led_info *led_info)
 {
--
2.43.0
